<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">HMS Bounty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Bounty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">HMS Bounty was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> ship sent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh">William Bligh</a> to collect breadfruit plants on Tahiti. In April 1789 part of the crew, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fletcher-christian">Fletcher Christian</a>, mutinied and set Bligh adrift; some mutineers settled on Pitcairn Island, while others stayed on Tahiti, where they served chiefs such as Tu, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-i">Pōmare I</a>. Bligh sailed some 6,700 kilometres in an open boat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hms-bounty.jpg" alt="HMS Bounty" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dan Kasberger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HMS_BOUNTY_II_with_Full_Sails.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Bounty was originally the collier Bethia, reportedly built in 1784 at Hull. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> bought her on 23 May 1787, refitted her and renamed her. She was small, at 215 tons, but full-rigged on three masts. The purchase was made solely for a botanical mission: to collect breadfruit plants in Tahiti and carry them to the British West Indies as a cheap food for the region's slaves. The naturalist Sir Joseph Banks promoted the idea and recommended Lieutenant William Bligh to the Admiralty. Bligh, aged 33, was appointed on 16 August 1787. He had served as sailing master of the sloop Resolution on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>'s third voyage. The complement was 46 men, with Bligh the only commissioned officer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Bounty sailed from Spithead on 23 December 1787. After a month of trying to round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-horn">Cape Horn</a> in adverse weather, Bligh turned east, around the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. The ship reached Tahiti on 26 October 1788, and the crew spent five months collecting 1,015 breadfruit plants, living ashore and taking Tahitian customs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fletcher-christian">Fletcher Christian</a> married a Tahitian woman, Maimiti.</p><p>Bounty left Tahiti on 4 April 1789. On 28 April, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, Christian led a mutiny that took the ship without bloodshed. Of the 42 men aboard besides Bligh and Christian, 22 joined the mutineers, two were passive and 18 stayed loyal. Bligh and those who joined him were put into the ship's boat, and after an attack on Tofua killed one of them, Bligh sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlement of Coupang, reaching it after 47 days. Sixteen mutineers stayed in Tahiti, while Christian sailed on with eight other crewmen, six Tahitian men and eleven women, and reached Pitcairn Island on 15 January 1790. Bounty was burned there on 23 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>HMS Pandora, sent to capture the mutineers, arrived in March 1791 and took fourteen men within two weeks. She was wrecked on the Great Barrier Reef on 29 August 1791, with 35 lives lost. The Pitcairn settlers stayed unseen until February 1808, when the sealer Topaz found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, the last surviving mutineer, with the Tahitian women and their children. Bligh's log was used as evidence in the trials. Bligh is often portrayed as an abusive captain, but Caroline Alexander argued in 2003 that he was lenient by the standards of the time. Luis Marden found remains of the ship off Pitcairn in January 1957.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “HMS Bounty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Bligh’s HMS <b>Bounty</b> anchored in Matavai Bay in October 1788, and after the mutiny of April 1789 sixteen of its men returned to live on Tahiti.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mutineers seized <b>HMS Bounty</b> and set Captain William Bligh adrift with 18 men in an open launch.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fletcher Christian and eight other mutineers from <b>HMS Bounty</b>, with Tahitian men and women, landed on uninhabited Pitcairn in 1790 and burned the ship.</blockquote><a href="/state/pitcairn-islands">Pitcairn Islands</a></li></ul></section>
